<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tukaram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1650">1650</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Tukoba, Tukobaraya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tukaram was a 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> poet-saint of the Varkari tradition, born at Dehu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and devoted to the god Vithoba, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. In his abhangas, devotional poems in a simple folk style, he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> hierarchy, mechanical ritual and the misconduct of certain religious leaders, which brought him opponents such as a local monastery head, Mambaji Gosavi. Tradition credits him with some 4,500 abhangas, collected in the Tukaram Gatha, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> were reworked for about two centuries after his death. Among his disciples was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> woman, Bahinabai.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tukaram.jpg" alt="Tukaram" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anant Shivaji Desai, Ravi Varma Press · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tukaram_by_Raja_Ravi_Varma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
